# A SEQUENTIAL `for i in 0..<N:` is refused deliberately, and stays refused.
# `0..<N` exists so `parallel for` keeps a statically-shaped iteration space to
# chunk across threads; accepting it in a plain `for` would make it `range()`
# under a new name and leave the three-clause form with nothing to do
# (plan.md's Pre-flight, phase 5). Refused at src/tychoc.c:3472 with
#
#     error: `0..<N` counts only in a `parallel for` --
#            write `for i := 0; i < N; i += 1:` for a sequential count
#
# naming the replacement form, with the loop variable substituted in.
#
# WAS tests/reject/dotlt_no_grammar.ty, written by phase 3 when `..<` lexed
# (TK_DOTLT, src/tychoc.c:529) but had no grammar at all. Renamed here because
# the token now HAS a grammar and only this use of it is illegal. Phase 3's
# refusal came from the foreach branch parsing `0` as the collection and then
# demanding the block colon ("expected ':' before the block"); what phase 5
# changes is the message, not the verdict. Note it never died in the LEXER even
# before phase 3 -- `0..<3` lexed as INT '.' '.' '<' INT and was refused at the
# postfix-field site.
#
# This lane asserts only that the refusal is loud: nonzero exit plus a non-empty
# diagnostic (tests/run.sh:170-185). The EXACT text is locked byte-for-byte by
# the golden at tests/diag/dotlt_sequential.err.
fn main():
    for i in 0..<3:
        print(str(i))
